---
import LegalPage from "../components/LegalPage.astro";

const sections = [
  ["introduction", "Introduction"],
  ["reporting", "Reporting a vulnerability"],
  ["scope", "Scope"],
  ["out-of-scope", "Out of scope"],
  ["safe-harbor", "Safe harbour"],
  ["bounty", "No bug bounty"],
  ["practices", "How the service is secured"],
  ["responsibilities", "Your responsibilities"],
  ["updates", "Updates to this policy"],
  ["contact", "Contact"],
] as const;
---

<LegalPage
  title="Security Policy — RAS Code"
  description="How to report a vulnerability in RAS Code or RAS Connect, what is in scope, and the safe harbour for good-faith research."
  heading="RAS Code Security Policy"
  lede="RAS Code runs on your machines and RAS Connect is operated by one person. This policy explains how to report a vulnerability, what is in scope, and what protects you when you research in good faith."
  effectiveDate="2026-08-28"
  lastUpdated="2026-08-28"
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="introduction">
    <p class="section-number">01</p>
    <h2>Introduction</h2>
    <p>
      This policy covers the RAS Code source code, the desktop, web, and mobile clients built from
      it, the RAS Connect relay operated by Richard Solomou, and the RAS Code website.
    </p>
    <p>
      RAS Connect is a control plane. It links your devices to a RAS Code server you run yourself and
      delivers notifications; it is not in the path of your normal traffic, and it never receives your
      prompts, source code, or agent output. Most of the execution path belongs to you: your machine,
      your network, your repositories, and the agent providers you configure. Security here is
      genuinely shared.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="reporting">
    <p class="section-number">02</p>
    <h2>Reporting a Vulnerability</h2>
    <p>
      Email hi@ras.sh. Please do not open a public issue or
      pull request for a security problem, and do not disclose it publicly until there has been a
      reasonable opportunity to fix it.
    </p>
    <p>Include, where you can:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>What the issue is and what an attacker could do with it;</li>
      <li>The affected component, version, endpoint, or URL;</li>
      <li>Steps to reproduce, or a minimal proof of concept;</li>
      <li>Relevant requests, responses, or logs, with secrets removed; and</li>
      <li>How you would like to be credited, if at all.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Reports are handled by one person, not a security team. Expect acknowledgement within a few
      days rather than the same day. You will be told what the assessment is and when a fix ships.
      Serious issues in the hosted service are prioritised over everything else; issues in the client
      software are fixed and released on the normal release cadence.
    </p>
    <p>
      Never include a password, API key, access token, or private key in a report. If you found an
      exposed secret, say where it is and revoke it if it is yours; do not paste it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="scope">
    <p class="section-number">03</p>
    <h2>Scope</h2>
    <p>The following are in scope:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The RAS Connect relay and its HTTP API, including authentication, token exchange, proof
        verification, and replay protection;</li>
      <li>Environment linking, credential issuance, and the authorisation boundaries between one
        account's environments and another's;</li>
      <li>Managed endpoint provisioning, including anything that lets one account reach or affect
        another account's tunnel or hostname;</li>
      <li>Notification and Live Activity delivery, including anything that leaks activity data to the
        wrong device or account;</li>
      <li>The RAS Code server, its WebSocket protocol, its pairing and token handling, and its
        provider adapters;</li>
      <li>The desktop, web, and mobile clients, including credential storage and deep-link handling;
        and</li>
      <li>The RAS Code website.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="out-of-scope">
    <p class="section-number">04</p>
    <h2>Out of Scope</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Infrastructure operated by Cloudflare, Clerk, Apple, Google, Expo, or PostHog. Report
        those to the provider concerned;</li>
      <li>Coding agents, model providers, and any other third-party service you connect to RAS Code;</li>
      <li>The consequences of a coding agent doing what it was asked to do, including destructive
        commands you approved;</li>
      <li>A RAS Code server deliberately exposed to an untrusted network by its operator;</li>
      <li>Denial-of-service, volumetric, brute-force, and load testing of any kind;</li>
      <li>Social engineering, phishing, or physical attacks against anyone;</li>
      <li>Findings from automated scanners with no demonstrated impact, missing security headers with
        no exploit path, and best-practice observations; and</li>
      <li>Issues that require a compromised device, a malicious operating system, or physical access
        to a machine you do not own.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="safe-harbor">
    <p class="section-number">05</p>
    <h2>Safe Harbour</h2>
    <p>
      Good-faith security research is welcome. If you follow this policy, your research is authorised:
      no legal action will be initiated against you over it, no complaint will be made to your
      employer or to law enforcement, and accidental good-faith breaches of this policy will be
      treated as accidents. If a third party takes action over research that complied with this
      policy, that authorisation will be made known where it is appropriate to do so.
    </p>
    <p>To stay within the safe harbour:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Test only accounts, environments, and data that are yours, or that you have explicit
        permission to test;</li>
      <li>Access only the minimum needed to demonstrate the issue;</li>
      <li>Stop immediately if you reach another person's data, and report it rather than exploring
        further;</li>
      <li>Do not degrade, disrupt, or overload the service, and do not modify or delete data that is
        not yours;</li>
      <li>Do not use social engineering, phishing, physical attacks, or denial-of-service testing;</li>
      <li>Do not test third-party infrastructure listed as out of scope; and</li>
      <li>Give a reasonable opportunity to fix the issue before disclosing it, and coordinate the
        timing.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This safe harbour cannot authorise anything unlawful, and it does not extend to activity outside
      the scope set out above.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="bounty">
    <p class="section-number">06</p>
    <h2>No Bug Bounty</h2>
    <p>
      There is no bug bounty and no reward programme. RAS Connect is free, run by one person, and has
      no budget for payouts. Reports are still very welcome, and credit in the fix commit, the release
      notes, or this page is offered gladly if you want it. Please do not send a report conditional on
      payment.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="practices">
    <p class="section-number">07</p>
    <h2>How the Service Is Secured</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Minimal data.</strong> The relay is deliberately kept out of the traffic path. Your
        prompts, code, files, and agent output never reach it. Only the records described in the
        <a href="/privacy-policy">Privacy Policy</a> are stored, and activity text is truncated to
        short fixed lengths.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Short-lived, bound credentials.</strong> Credentials for connecting a client to an
        environment expire in minutes and are bound to the requesting client's key, so a stolen
        credential cannot be replayed from elsewhere. Only a hash of each credential is stored, and
        credentials can be revoked.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Proof of possession and replay protection.</strong> Sensitive relay endpoints require
        a signed proof of possession, and used proof identifiers are recorded until they expire so
        that a proof cannot be replayed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Separate trust boundaries.</strong> The relay and your RAS Code server hold separate
        credentials and verify each other independently. Environments publish activity under their own
        signed proofs.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Encrypted transport.</strong> All connections to the relay use TLS. Authorization
        credentials are kept out of traces, and delivery records store only a short trailing fragment
        of a push token instead of the token.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Limited access.</strong> Production systems and operational data are accessible only
        to the operator, through the providers' own authenticated consoles.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Open source.</strong> The relay and the clients are public, so you can read exactly
        what is stored and what is sent, and you can run your own relay instead.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These measures reduce risk; they do not eliminate it. No guarantee is made that the software or
      the service is free of vulnerabilities.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="responsibilities">
    <p class="section-number">08</p>
    <h2>Your Responsibilities</h2>
    <p>Most of the attack surface is on your side of the line. You are responsible for:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Keeping RAS Code, your operating systems, and your connected tools up to date;</li>
      <li>Securing the machines your RAS Code servers run on, and the networks they are reachable
        from;</li>
      <li>Protecting your account, provider, and repository credentials, and using multi-factor
        authentication where it is available;</li>
      <li>Choosing your connection mode knowingly: a managed endpoint routes your traffic through
        Cloudflare, whereas a local network or self-operated tunnel does not;</li>
      <li>Reviewing commands, patches, and file changes an agent proposes before applying or running
        them;</li>
      <li>Giving agents and integrations only the access they need; and</li>
      <li>Unlinking environments and removing devices you no longer use or no longer control.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="updates">
    <p class="section-number">09</p>
    <h2>Updates to This Policy</h2>
    <p>
      This policy will be revised as the software and the infrastructure change. Revisions are posted
      at this URL with updated dates above. Changes to the reporting process, the scope, or the safe
      harbour apply from their effective date onwards, and never retroactively to research already
      carried out in good faith.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="contact">
    <p class="section-number">10</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Security reports: hi@ras.sh.
    </p>
    <p>
      For questions about the <a href="/terms-of-service">Terms of Service</a> or the
      <a href="/privacy-policy">Privacy Policy</a>, use the contact addresses given in those
      documents.
    </p>
  </section>
</LegalPage>
